  "OverallSummary": "This case involves Victor Alan Lessinger, a General Securities Representative formerly associated with Colorado Financial Service Corporation, who violated Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) Care Obligation by recommending unsuitable high-risk investments to an elderly customer. Between June 2022 and February 2023, Lessinger recommended that an 82-year-old customer with limited investment experience, low income (less than $25,000 annually), moderate risk tolerance, and a net worth between $50,000 and $99,999 invest in three high-risk closed-end funds at different times. These funds, which invested in CLOs and below investment grade securities, constituted concentrations of at least 28%, 23%, and 37% of the customer's net worth respectively. The analysis revealing the Care Obligation violation included: (1) assessment of the customer's investment profile (age, limited finances, moderate risk tolerance, limited experience); (2) evaluation of the recommended securities' risk level (prospectuses described them as highly speculative with significant risk of loss); (3) determination that the concentration levels (28-37% of net worth) created excessive risk; and (4) conclusion that these recommendations were not in the customer's best interest. As a result of these unsuitable investments, the customer lost $5,029.85. FINRA determined that Lessinger willfully violated Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(1) and FINRA Rule 2010. The sanctions include a three-month suspension, a $5,000 fine, and restitution of $5,029.85 plus interest to the customer. Lessinger had prior regulatory history, including a 2000 AWC for allowing an unqualified representative to effect securities transactions and a 2005 SEC judgment for failing to supervise brokers conducting penny stock manipulation."
